-- emit: `==` on a `List`, which is the one `eq` §9 does not derive
-- (`docs/design/static-dispatch-spike.md` §9.5, §5.2, A.55, A.60, A.51).
--
-- `List a` is a `foreign type`: it has no constructors, so there is no body
-- to derive from, and §9.5's loop lives in `core/List.js` behind the
-- `pub foreign eq` §5.2 gives it. So both calls here are `List$eq`, and
-- what tells them apart is the hidden leading argument of §8.1 — the
-- ELEMENT's own `eq`, which is the whole of what a `where` clause buys.
--
-- `List Int` hands it `eq$prim` by bare name: the primitive comparator of
-- §9.1 is already a function of two arguments. `List { x : Int }` hands it
-- the record shape's `eq$r$x`, which is a function of THREE — its one
-- field's evidence, then the two records — so it is the eta-expansion of
-- §8.2 and not the bare name. Those two lines are the difference between
-- an evidence value and a name.
--
-- Before §5.2 landed, both were `Basics$eq` — `core/Basics.js`'s one
-- structural walk, taken through A.51's bridge because every part of these
-- two comparisons says the walk would answer the same thing — and `eq$r$x`
-- sat here uncalled. The case the walk gets WRONG, an element with a
-- `pub eq` of its own, was refused; it now runs, and
-- `tests/corpus/run/ListElementEq.beni` is what says so.
import Node exposing (Program)


pub sameInts : List Int, List Int -> Bool
sameInts a b =
    a == b


pub sameRows : List { x : Int }, List { x : Int } -> Bool
sameRows a b =
    a == b


main : Program
main =
    Node.printLines []
